{"major":{"id":"UCLA-LINGUISTICSBA","name":"Linguistics BA","kind":"major","catalog_year":"2026-27","source":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/major/2026/LINGUISTICSBA","sections":[{"name":"The Major","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 103","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 120A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 120B","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 120C","status":"inactive"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING C104","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 105","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 110","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 130","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 132","status":"inactive"}],"count":2,"label":"Additional Linguistics Electives","notes":"Select two courses from:"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 165A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 165B","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 165C","status":"inactive"}],"count":2,"label":"Linguistics 165 series","notes":"Select two courses from:"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"ASL 131","status":"active"},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"text","text":"Linguistics 100 through 199, minimum of 4 units"}],"label":"Linguistics"}],"count":3,"label":"Upper-Division Electives","notes":"Select three upper-division elective courses from the Linguistics Department (minimum 4 graded units each) from the following. No more than one course from 197, 198A, 198B, or 199 may be applied."}],"label":"The Major"}},{"name":"Honors Program","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 198A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 198B","status":"inactive"}],"label":"Linguistics 198A and 198B"},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 199","status":"inactive"}],"label":"Linguistics 199"}],"label":"Honors Program","notes":"Departmental honors are awarded at graduation to those students who have a grade-point average of 3.6 or better in their junior and senior years and who have received a grade of A in Linguistics 198A and 198B or in 199. Qualified students may be proposed by any member of the faculty to the faculty as a whole for the award of highest honors on the basis of a piece of research in linguistics completed at UCLA."}},{"name":"Computing Specialization","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10A","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10B","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10C","status":"active"}],"label":"Program in Computing"},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 31","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 32","status":"active"}],"label":"Computer Science"}],"count":1,"label":"Introduction to Computing","notes":"Select one series from:"},{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 185A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"MATH 61","status":"inactive"}],"label":"Linguistics 185A and Mathematics 61"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING C104","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 127","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 132","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 165A","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 165B","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 165C","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 180","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"LING 185B","status":"inactive"}],"count":1,"label":"Additional Linguistics Elective","notes":"Select one course from:"}],"label":"Computing Specialization","notes":"Students may select a specialization in Computing by satisfying all the requirements for a bachelor’s degree in the specified major and completing the following courses. Students graduate with a bachelor’s degree in their major and a specialization in Computing."}},{"name":"Preparation for the Major","requirement":{"type":"all","items":[{"type":"course","code":"LING 20","status":"inactive"},{"type":"choose","items":[{"type":"course","code":"ANTHRO 4","status":"active"},{"type":"course","code":"PHILOS 31","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"PSYCH 10","status":"inactive"},{"type":"course","code":"PSYCH 100A","status":"inactive"}],"count":2,"label":"Electives","notes":"Select two courses from the following list. Only one course from Psychology 10 or 100A may be applied."},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the equivalent to the sixth level of one foreign language or the equivalent to the third level of two different foreign languages."}],"label":"Preparation for the Major"}}],"narrative":[],"overrides_applied":[]},"nodes":[{"id":"ANTHRO 4","title":"Culture and Communication","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"elective","groups":["Preparation for the Major: Electives"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, one hour. Required as preparation for both bachelor’s degrees. Introduction to study of communication from anthropological perspective. Formal linguistic methods compared with ethnographically oriented methods focused on context-bound temporal unfolding of communicative activities. Topics include language in everyday life and ritual events, socialization, literacy, multilingualism, miscommunication, political discourse, and art-making as cultural activity. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/ANTHRO4"},{"id":"ASL 1","title":"Elementary American Sign Language","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":false,"role":"prerequisite","groups":[],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, five hours. Introduction to fundamentals of American sign language. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/ASL1"},{"id":"ASL 131","title":"Structure of American Sign Language","units":"4","offered":[],"level":"upper","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"elective","groups":["The Major: Upper-Division Electives"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 1 or Linguistics 20","requires":{"type":"or","items":[{"type":"course","code":"ASL 1","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},{"type":"course","code":"LING 20","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null}]},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, two and one half hours. Advanced overview of the linguistic analysis, research methods, and topics of American Sign Language. Major topics include phonology, morphology, syntax, language use, and linguistic applications. Examination of some comparisons with English and other spoken and signed languages. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/ASL131"},{"id":"COM SCI 31","title":"Introduction to Computer Science I","units":"4","offered":["Fall"],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Computing Specialization: Computer Science"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, four hours; discussion, two hours; outside study, six hours. Introduction to computer science via theory, applications, and programming. Basic data types, operators and control structures. Input/output. Procedural and data abstraction. Introduction to object-oriented software development. Functions, recursion. Arrays, strings, pointers. Abstract data types, object-oriented programming. Examples and exercises from computer science theory and applications. Letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMSCI31"},{"id":"COM SCI 32","title":"Introduction to Computer Science II","units":"4","offered":["Fall"],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Computing Specialization: Computer Science"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 31","requires":{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 31","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, four hours; discussion, two hours; outside study, six hours. Object-oriented software development. Abstract data type definition and use. Overloading, inheritance, polymorphism. Object-oriented view of data structures: stacks, queues, lists. Algorithm analysis. Trees, graphs, and associated algorithms. Searching and sorting. Case studies and exercises from computer science applications. Letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMSCI32"},{"id":"COMPTNG 10A","title":"Introduction to Programming","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, two hours; laboratory, eight hours. No prior programming experience assumed. Basic principles of programming, using C&plus;&plus;; algorithmic, procedural problem solving; program design and development; basic data types, control structures and functions; functional arrays and pointers; introduction to classes for programmer-defined data types. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG10A"},{"id":"COMPTNG 10B","title":"Intermediate Programming","units":"4","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing"],"column":1,"prereq_text":"course 10A or Computer Science 31","requires":{"type":"or","items":[{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10A","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},{"type":"course","code":"COM SCI 31","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null}]},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, one hour; laboratory, eight hours. Object oriented programming in C++; operator overloading; memory management, copy and move constructors, copy and move assignment operators, destructors; iterators; data structures and their implementation, linked lists, binary search trees; inheritance and polymorphism; recursion, algorithms for sorting and searching. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG10B"},{"id":"COMPTNG 10C","title":"Advanced Programming","units":"5","offered":[],"level":"lower","status":"active","in_major":true,"role":"choice","groups":["Computing Specialization: Program in Computing"],"column":2,"prereq_text":"course 10B","requires":{"type":"course","code":"COMPTNG 10B","concurrent":false,"min_grade":null},"ambiguous":false,"description":"Lecture, three hours; discussion, two hours; laboratory, eight hours. More advanced algorithms and data structuring techniques; additional emphasis on algorithmic efficiency; advanced features of C&plus;&plus;, such as inheritance and virtual functions; graph algorithms. P/NP or letter grading.","url":"https://catalog.registrar.ucla.edu/course/2026/COMPTNG10C"},{"id":"LING 20","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"required","groups":["Preparation for the Major"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"LING 103","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"required","groups":["The Major"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"LING 105","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"elective","groups":["The Major: Additional Linguistics Electives"],"column":0,"prereq_text":"","requires":null,"ambiguous":false,"description":""},{"id":"LING 110","title":"","units":"","offered":[],"level":"","status":"inactive","in_major":true,"role":"elective","groups":["The Major: Additional Linguistics Electives"],"column":0,"prereq_